`context/instructions/browser-tools.md` states the capabilities a driver needs to
fit SDT's protocol (`context/instructions/browser.md`) and how to choose between
the shapes available. The profiles are **capability classes, not products**: a
project may use any driver with the same capabilities, and the choice is recorded
in its own `context/instructions/project.md` (library-first, see
`context/instructions/development.md`).

## How to choose

- **Layout truth** (pixel geometry, computed styles, both color schemes) → a
  driver over a **real browser engine**. Anything that owns its own layout
  engine is not an oracle for pixel geometry, however good its rendering looks.
- **Read-only extraction / eval / parallel scrape** (no layout assertion) → a
  self-contained engine that owns the DOM and runs JS: cheaper, and enough when
  no pixel claim is made.
- **Neither present** → degrade: report the render step as **SKIPPED** (see
  `browser.md`), never PASSED; do not install a driver unprompted.
- **CLI over MCP when both exist** → prefer a CLI invocation: it is more
  token-efficient than an MCP tool, because it avoids loading a large tool schema
  and verbose accessibility trees into context. MCP stays useful for
  long-running loops that need persistent state and rich introspection.
- Every shape follows the wait-by-outcome discipline and the
  pixel-diff-is-a-tripwire rule from `browser.md`.

## What a layout driver must provide

Check these capabilities against the driver's own help before adopting it; the
command behind each one is the project's to record.

- **Real engine**: drives Chrome/Chromium (or another real engine) over CDP, so
  computed styles, fonts and rasterisation are the browser's own.
- **Snapshot with stable refs**: a compact accessibility tree whose element refs
  survive between commands, so a click can name a node without a CSS guess.
- **Annotated screenshot**: numbered labels that map 1:1 onto refs, so a visual
  claim can be traced to an element.
- **Structural and pixel diff**: a threshold and a dimension-mismatch check
  between two runs.
- **Viewport and device control**: `set viewport` / `set device` and a colour
  scheme, so both schemes can be checked.
- **Geometry and style reads**: the box and the computed styles of one element,
  which is what a layout assertion actually needs.
- **Accessibility audit**: an axe-compatible scan without a CDN dependency.
- **Performance vitals**: LCP/CLS/TTFB/FCP/INP when a budget is claimed.
- **Bounded output and action policy**: a cap on returned bytes, an allowed-domain
  list and per-category action confirmation, so a page cannot flood the context
  or drive an unintended action.
- **A persistent session with an idle timeout**: one daemon or process reused
  across commands, stopping when idle; a cold start per command makes a
  multi-step check unusably slow.

## What a text-only driver costs

An in-page or text-DOM agent can navigate and fill forms, but it cannot measure
pixel geometry and it usually cannot inspect a page it did not author. For a
layout claim the render step therefore stays **SKIPPED** (see `browser.md`), not
PASSED.

## Version scope

- Bind to the installed driver, not to this profile: run the tool's own help or
  CLI-served skills (`--help`, `skills get ...`) and re-verify flags before
  relying on them. Drivers evolve quickly; this file is a starting point, not a
  frozen contract.
- No install recipe lives here. A project that adopts a driver records how it is
  installed and invoked in its own `context/instructions/project.md`.
